The inline lookup takes the x87 and SSE mode bits of the TB flags from the
TB it is leaving, as constants, instead of rebuilding them from env at
every indirect jump.

Patch 20's gen_lookup_and_goto_ptr computes pc / cs_base / flags as TCG
ops, the way cpu_get_tb_cpu_state does, so a ret, a call through a
pointer and a jmp through a register find the next TB without leaving
generated code. The flags half was ~22 host instructions of a ~70
instruction lookup: hflags and eflags, then the x87 mode byte, the SSE
mode and its sticky inexact flag, and (patch 37) the x87 inexact flag.
A C++ engine's virtual calls and small methods are mostly that: in
3DMark 99's first-person test MAX-FX's core DLL spent its time on `ret`
and `call *0x84(%eax)`, ~100 host instructions each.

Three of those terms cannot differ from the leaving TB's own flags:

  - the x87 mode (bits 29-30): every instruction that can change the
    control word ends its TB (gen_x87s_ends_tb);
  - the SSE mode bit (31): ldmxcsr, fxrstor and xrstor end their TB, so a
    set bit stays set; one that became set during the TB (PE turning
    sticky) is emitted as 0, which picks the exact variant of the target
    until the next full lookup -- slower, never wrong;
  - the x87 PE bit (2): the TB's own while no fclex ran in it
    (x87s_pe_sticky), else 0, by the same argument.

They are one constant OR now. hflags and the eflags bits are still
loaded: RF, TF and the interrupt shadow are what gen_eob has just left
them, and a far call or jump reaches the same lookup through a helper
that may change CS and CPL.

Measured on 3DMark 99 Max, claude98, 800x600x16, after patch 37: CPU
3DMarks 14690 -> 15389 (+5 %), the first-person test 16.2 -> 17.1 fps,
3DMarks 5917 -> 5932. x87 and SSE batteries identical on/off, SMC and rep
unchanged, scripts/test.sh all 57/57.

Behind inline-lookup, like the function it changes.

